 # Design analysis for combo D5/0 + D11/1
-# a) Concrete behavior:
-#    The base task can have 1-5 frames and any frame may emit only one useful beam. Here the grammar is fixed:
-#    exactly two 4x4 frames, each enclosing a 2x2 block and exposing exactly two structural border gaps.
-#    A concrete template is "upper frame emits E+SE, lower frame emits E+NE". In the base task that scene
-#    would often reduce to two unrelated single beams; in this combo it creates four seeds, two early 45-degree
-#    meetings, and then redirected zigzags that keep interacting farther away from the frames.
+# a) Concrete behavior trace:
+#    The base task allowed 1-5 framed 2x2 blocks and each frame could expose 1-4 structural gaps.
+#    Here the arena is tightened to exactly two 4x4 frames, and each frame must expose exactly two
+#    gaps, so the input always launches four beams. A concrete template used by the generator is:
+#    - left frame gaps at local (2,3) and (3,3), launching E and SE;
+#    - right frame gaps at local (3,0) and (3,2), launching SW and S.
+#    In the original task one block could act almost independently. In this fused version the two
+#    diagonal beams first cross, then the descendant cardinal/diagonal traces collide again, so the
+#    solver has to follow a 4-beam interaction network instead of isolated single exits.
 # b) Complexity calibration:
-#    Four beams are enough to create dense interactions without making the scene unreadable. The object types
-#    stay unchanged: one shared frame color, two block colors, and only four missing wall cells. Humans can
-#    still count the sources directly from the input while the output is visibly denser than the original task.
-# c) Hidden-assumption audit of the original solver:
-#    - _infer_frame_color assumes one unique wall color around each block. The generator keeps one shared wall color.
-#    - _frame_gaps_for_block can return many structural gaps in the base task. The new main() asserts exactly two.
-#    - resolve_cell_collision only supports 45-degree and 90-degree meetings. The generator therefore samples only
-#      inward-facing family layouts and rejects any candidate whose simulation would produce a forbidden head-on case.
-#    - The simulation assumes beams step only through background. The generator preserves >=2-cell frame spacing and
-#      validates every candidate by running the same simulation used by main().
+#    Fixing the frame count to two removes source-count uncertainty, but the added second gap per
+#    frame more than compensates: four simultaneous beams guarantee a dense middle interaction zone.
+#    The generator keeps to a small family of parity-controlled templates and rotates them, so the
+#    pattern is visibly different from the base task without exploding into too many case splits.
+# c) Hidden-assumption audit:
+#    - The original `main()` only assumed "at least one 2x2 block" and "each block has >=1 gap".
+#      Those assumptions are now invalid: the fused rule depends on exactly two blocks and exactly
+#      two structural gaps per block, so the solver asserts both explicitly.
+#    - The collision code assumes beams only hit background or previously blocked collision cells.
+#      Multi-gap frames increase the risk of a beam punching into the other frame, so the generator
+#      constructs layouts with even frame separation and then rejects any sample whose simulation
+#      still violates the free-runway assumption.
+#    - `find_partner()` groups adjacent lockstep beams. Under this combo that grouping becomes common
+#      rather than incidental, because every sample starts with two 2-beam source families.
 # d) Placement legality:
-#    Both frames are stamped as non-overlapping 4x4 boxes with the original spacing rule. Family templates keep the
-#    emitting sides pointed toward a large open region, and _rays_clear() enforces several free cells of runway.
+#    The generator uses two non-overlapping 4x4 frames on a shared row, separated by an even number
+#    of columns so the inward diagonals meet on valid lattice parity. The whole grid is then rotated
+#    by 0/90/180/270 degrees, which preserves in-bounds placement and the >=2-cell frame clearance.
 # e) Visual observability:
-#    The invariant "two green frames, two holes per frame" is directly visible in every input. The corresponding
-#    output always contains a four-ray interaction web rather than isolated beams, so the combo behavior can be
-#    inferred without consulting color ids or any hidden ordering.
+#    Human solvers can now rely on three visible invariants across examples: there are always exactly
+#    two frames, each frame has exactly two missing border pixels, and the four resulting beams always
+#    create at least two distinct collisions. No invisible color ordering is used.
 # f) Visual cleanliness:
-#    The generator uses only one interaction family at a time and pushes both frames toward one side of the grid,
