    10	# Design analysis for combo D5/0 + D11/1
    11	# a) Concrete behavior trace:
    12	#    The base task allowed 1-5 framed 2x2 blocks and each frame could expose 1-4 structural gaps.
    13	#    Here the arena is tightened to exactly two 4x4 frames, and each frame must expose exactly two
    14	#    gaps, so the input always launches four beams. A concrete template used by the generator is:
    15	#    - left frame gaps at local (0,3) and (3,3), launching NE and SE;
    16	#    - right frame gaps at local (0,0) and (3,1), launching NW and S.
    17	#    In the original task one block could act almost independently. In this fused version the two
    18	#    diagonal beams first cross, then the descendant cardinal/diagonal traces collide again, so the
    19	#    solver has to follow a 4-beam interaction network instead of isolated single exits.
    20	# b) Complexity calibration:
    21	#    Fixing the frame count to two removes source-count uncertainty, but the added second gap per
    22	#    frame more than compensates: four simultaneous beams guarantee a dense middle interaction zone.
    23	#    The generator keeps to a small family of parity-controlled templates and rotates them, so the
    24	#    pattern is visibly different from the base task without exploding into too many case splits.
    25	# c) Hidden-assumption audit:
    26	#    - The original `main()` only assumed "at least one 2x2 block" and "each block has >=1 gap".
    27	#      Those assumptions are now invalid: the fused rule depends on exactly two blocks and exactly
    28	#      two structural gaps per block, so the solver asserts both explicitly.
    29	#    - The collision code assumes beams only hit background or previously blocked collision cells.
    30	#      Multi-gap frames increase the risk of a beam punching into the other frame, so the generator
    31	#      constructs layouts with even frame separation and then rejects any sample whose simulation
    32	#      still violates the free-runway assumption.
    33	#    - `find_partner()` groups adjacent lockstep beams. Under this combo that grouping becomes common
    34	#      rather than incidental, because every sample starts with two 2-beam source families.
    35	# d) Placement legality:
